{{define "ens_publish_overview"}}You are guiding a user through pointing an ENS/onchain name at IPFS content.

Why this is different from a normal website:
  - ENS names do NOT use the websites_create / DNS _dnslink system. A .eth (or
    other onchain) name resolves via an IPNS-based contenthash record set onchain
    in the ENS resolver.
  - Pinner publishes the content under a per-domain IPNS key via ens_point and
    returns the contenthash value; the user then sets that contenthash onchain
    from their own wallet or ENS manager (an onchain transaction Pinner cannot do).

Workflow overview (6 steps):
  1. auth_check  : confirm the user is authenticated
  2. content     : obtain a CID (upload if not already provided)
  3. point       : call ens_point with the ENS name + CID to publish + get the contenthash
  4. wallet      : guide the user to set the contenthash onchain (without assuming a wallet)
  5. verify      : open the returned gateway URL after the onchain tx confirms
  6. complete    : summarize

Rules:
  - Do not use websites_create for ENS pointing unless the user explicitly changes
    their mind to a normal (non-ENS) website.
  - ens_point and ens_unpoint are NOT on the default tools/list — discover them via
    search_tools with query "ens".
  - Never assume which wallet or ENS manager the user has. Surface the exact
    contenthash value and present common options (ENS manager at app.ens.domains,
    the ENS SDK via ethers.js, a wallet with ENS support) as suggestions, and ask
    the user what they use.
  - The contenthash set is an onchain transaction the user signs; Pinner cannot set
    it and cannot see/sign the user's wallet. Do not invent a tool to "set the ENS
    record" server-side.
  - Verification uses the returned verify_url (eth.limo for .eth names). It can only
    reflect the new content after the onchain contenthash transaction confirms and
    the resolver propagates.{{if .ENSName}}

ENS name: {{.ENSName}}{{end}}{{end}}

{{define "ens_publish_auth_status"}}Read the pinner://account/status resource above. If "authenticated" is false, instruct the user to authenticate first. Do not proceed until authentication is confirmed.
{{end}}

{{define "ens_publish_content_upload"}}Step: content
No CID was provided. Upload the content first to obtain a publishable directory CID:
  - For a static website bundle (ZIP containing index.html, CSS, JS, images, nested pages):
    call upload_file and treat it as a single directory DAG (archive_mode=convert). Verify
    index.html is at the archive root, not wrapped in a parent directory.
  - For a single HTML file intended as a website: use wrap=true and do NOT set an explicit
    name (auto-named to index.html so it resolves at root).
  - Use the byte route your host supports (upload_file with the host file / local path /
    mint+PUT, or upload_url / upload_data per capabilities).
The upload returns a CID that is already pinned. Keep it for the point step.
{{end}}

{{define "ens_publish_content_cid"}}Step: content
A CID is provided: {{.CID}}. If it is not a pinned directory CID with index.html at the root
for a website, note that and treat it as arbitrary content. Either way, keep the CID for the
point step.
{{end}}

{{define "ens_publish_point"}}Step: point
Discover the tool: call search_tools with query "ens" and resolve ens_point via
describe_tool. Then call ens_point with:
  name: "{{.ENSName}}"
  cid:  "{{if .CID}}{{.CID}}{{else}}<the CID from the upload step>{{end}}"

ens_point creates or reuses the IPNS key for the ENS name, publishes the CID to it, and
returns:
  - contenthash : the ipns://<ipns-name> value to set onchain
  - verify_url  : a gateway URL to confirm resolution (eth.limo for .eth)
  - next_steps  : ordered guidance (which you adapt for this user)
It is idempotent: repointing the same or a new CID reuses the existing key.
{{end}}

{{define "ens_publish_wallet"}}Step: wallet
The contenthash value to set in the ENS resolver is the contenthash that ens_point
returned (it looks like ipns://<ipns-name>). Present this EXACT string to the user for
copying.

Explain in your own words, matching what the user is comfortable with:
  - Setting the contenthash is an onchain (Ethereum) transaction the user signs from their
    own wallet/ENS manager. Pinner cannot set it and never handles the user's private key.
  - Do NOT assume a specific wallet. Ask which they use, then offer the relevant path:
      * ENS Manager web app: app.ens.domains -> manage your name -> Records -> Content Hash
      * Any wallet with ENS support that exposes the name's Records editor
      * Programmatic: the ENS SDK / ethers.js setContentHash on the resolver contract
  - A strong ENS contenthash uses the ipns:// scheme (as returned); do not suggest a CID
    contenthash unless the user explicitly wants an immutable ipfs:// pointer.
Only proceed to verification AFTER the user confirms they have set (or will set) the
contenthash onchain.
{{end}}

{{define "ens_publish_verify"}}Step: verify
After the onchain contenthash transaction confirms (and the resolver propagates — this can
take a minute or two), verify at the verify_url that ens_point returned (for a .eth name this
is the eth.limo gateway, e.g. https://<name>.eth.limo). Open it or share it with the user and
confirm the content resolves. If it does not immediately, it is propagation lag, not failure —
re-check shortly.
{{end}}

{{define "ens_publish_complete"}}Step: complete
Summarize the ENS publish result:
  - ENS name: {{.ENSName}}
  - IPNS contenthash set onchain (user-signed): the ipns:// value returned by ens_point
  - Verify URL: as returned by ens_point
  - Note: to stop the name resolving later, the user removes the IPNS key with ens_unpoint
    (destructive, needs human confirmation) AND should clear the contenthash in the ENS
    resolver.
{{end}}
